    "dynamic_instructions": "{\r\n  \"datasetView\": \"**Meet the Data**\\n\\nGoal: Explore your dataset. Pick what your model will predict.\\n\\nA record is all the data about one board game. In the table view, each record shows as one row. Scroll right to see every feature.\\n\\n1. Find the record count at the top of the table (\\\"51 rows\\\"). Write it as the total records in **My Data** in your guide.\\n2. Click **Cards** to look through the records one at a time.\\n3. Pick one label for your model to predict (**Family Game?**, **Strategy Game?** or **Highly Rated?**). Write it in **My Data**.\\n4. Count the records in each category of your label:\\n    - a. Click **Filter**. Choose your label and one category.\\n    - b. Read the count at the top (for example, \\\"20 of 51 rows\\\"). Write it in **My Data**.\\n    - c. Click **Filter**, then **Clear filter**. Repeat for each category.\\n5. Pick two features that could help predict your label (**Max Players**, **Play Time (min)**, **Min Age (years)** or **Year Era**). Do step 4 for each feature.\\n\\nYou are done when **My Data** has your label and two features, each with record counts.\\n\\nThink about: Who could use a model trained on this data?\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Overview**: \\n\\nThis dataset contains qualities about different board games.\\n\\n**Data Source**: \\n\\nBoardGameGeek Dataset on Board Games (Kaggle).\",\r\n  \"results\": \"What can you do on the **Testing** screen?\\n* Interact with your decision tree\\n* Examine the test data\\n* Trace a prediction\\n* Save your decision tree to your device or backpack\"\r\n}",
